%[text] %[text:anchor:T_288EC79D] # Ingenuity
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](file:..\\MainMenu.m)
%[text] 
%[text] The Ingenuity copter is a small autonomous helicopter that made history as the first powered, controlled flight on another planet, Mars. Its design results from a collaboration between the Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL) and NASA. This project was part of the Mars 2020 mission and was deployed on Mars by the Perseverance rover. On April 19th, 2021, it was the first helicopter to perform a controlled flight in the thin atmosphere of Mars. Since then, it completed a series of complex flights paving the way for future aerial exploration of other planets.
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Animated image of Ingenuity getting ready for take-off on Mars](text:image:1ce2)
%[text]{"align":"center"} Ingenuity copter blades start to spin, credit: [NASA/JPL-Caltech/ASU](https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/missions/ingenuity)
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"} %[text:anchor:M_597CA885]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4C8EC99A] ## **Before you get started**
%[text] 
%[text] This live script is intended to be used with the code visible and output inline. On the **View** tab of the MATLAB Toolstrip, in the **View** section, select **Output Inline**. Alternately, select **Output inline** using the icon ![Live Script Output Inline](text:image:0036) at the top right of the Live Editor pane.
%[text] ![Lightbulb mark](text:image:4d2f) Interacting with this live script will build some familiarity with MATLAB as concepts and commands are introduced together. If you need more instruction, consider taking [MATLAB Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted), a free 2-hour online tutorial that teaches the essentials of MATLAB.
%[text] ![Warning symbol](text:image:5864)   For an optimal experience, follow the instructions and steps in the given sequence. Proceed to a new section only after completing the preceding one. Some sections depend on variables created in prior sections and will generate errors if run out of order.
%[text] The ![Try this icon](text:image:64e2)  and  ![Exercise icon](text:image:847a) icons refer to two different types of interactive activities that you will find in this script. The ![Try this icon](text:image:2b4f)  usually indicates an interaction where you will explore the visualization of some concept introduced in this script. 
%[text] The ![Exercise icon](text:image:821e) interactions are designed to challenge your understanding of those concepts and may be used for grading and completion checks by your professor.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_34E32149] ## Introduction
%[text] 
%[text] Dimensional analysis is a powerful mathematical tool that allows engineers to study the consistency of physical equations, derive relationships between physical quantities, and reduce the number of variables involved in a problem. It will enable us to identify parameters that control physical systems and express complex relationships between them using dimensionless numbers. This method is used in engineering, physics, and other sciences to solve problems involving complex physical systems, and it has many practical applications.
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_98FE04AD] ### Units and dimensions
%[text] 
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:4748) **Definition.** Dimensions are physical quantities that can be measured. Every dimension can be defined based on a combination of fundamental dimensions: mass  $M${"altText":"M"}, time $T${"altText":"T"}, length $L${"altText":"L"}, electric current $I${"altText":"I"}, temperature $\\Theta${"altText":"\Theta"}, amount of substance $N${"altText":"N"} and luminous intensity $J${"altText":"J"}. For example, velocity is a physical quantity that can be measured, and its dimension can be expressed as $L\\cdot T^{-1}${"altText":"L\cdot T^{-1}"}.
%[text] 
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:99af) **Definition.** Units are arbitrary quantities that can be measured and correspond to a precise dimension measurement. For example, one mole is a unit of the amount of substance $N${"altText":"N"} used in the International System of Units (SI) that corresponds to $6.022\\cdot 10^{23}${"altText":"6.022\cdot 10^{23}"} elementary entities (such as atoms or molecules).
%[text] 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:3b7f) **Try.** Every dimension can be measured using many different units.
  %[control:button:8c1f]{"position":[1,2]}
unitInfo("Length")
%[text] 
%%
%[text] While the unit system is often different based on the local tradition and the technical field, in this script, we will use the international system of units for all our measurements and calculations.
%[text] 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:5af5) **Try.** Define the base unit system.
  %[control:button:408a]{"position":[1,2]}
SIunits = baseUnits('SI')
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_83FCF0D2] ## Ingenuity's design
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B8AE6FBE] ### Identify the physical variables
%[text] 
%[text] Ingenuity is a small helicopter deployed on planet Mars with the Perseverance rover. It was designed by JPL (Jet Propulsion Laboratory). The helicopter’s wingspan $D${"altText":"D"} is 1.2 m, its mass $m${"altText":"m"} is 1.6 kg. Mars' atmosphere is defined by its density $\\rho${"altText":"\rho"} and viscosity $\\mu${"altText":"\mu"}, while the acceleration due to the gravitational force is $g${"altText":"g"}, $\\omega${"altText":"\omega"} is the rotor's angular velocity, and the propeller's thrust is $F\_T${"altText":"F\_T"}. Note that here $m${"altText":"m"} refers to the helicopter's mass, while $M${"altText":"M"} refers to the dimension of mass.
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Illustration of operating condition of ingenuity copter](text:image:5224)
%[text] The thrust force of the helicopter can be fitted as a function of all other variables:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f(F,\\rho,g,\\omega,m,D) = 0${"altText":"f(F,\rho,g,\omega,m,D) = 0"}
%[text] A naive approach would characterize the helicopter by considering all six parameters  $(\\rho,g,\\omega,m,D,F)${"altText":"(\rho,g,\omega,m,D,F)"}. This could be done but would require numerous experiments, requiring too much time. Instead, we will perform a dimensional analysis to reduce the number of variables needed to characterize the system.
%[text] In the rest of this script, we are going to write each measurement as a value and units such as:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\rho = \\bar{\\rho} \\cdot \[\\text{units}\]${"altText":"\rho = \bar{\rho} \cdot \[\text{units}\]"}
%[text] The first step of the dimensional analysis is to identify the independent parameters and their fundamental dimension. 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:7d0c) **Try.** Symbolically define the density as $\\rho=\\bar\\rho\\cdot\[\\text{units of density}\]${"altText":"\rho=\bar\rho\cdot\[\text{units of density}\]"}.
% Define the measurement:
syms rho_bar g_bar omega_bar m_bar D_bar
syms F_bar
% Define the unit system
u = symunit;

rho = rho_bar * (u.kg/u.m^3)
  %[control:button:9cda]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:24a6) **Exercise.** Modify the following edit fields to add the units to the other parameters.
% Your code goes in the following edit fields:
g = g_bar * (1);  %[control:editfield:03f0]{"position":[14,15]}
omega = omega_bar * (1);  %[control:editfield:881e]{"position":[22,23]}
m = m_bar * (1); %[control:editfield:3640]{"position":[14,15]}
D = D_bar * (11); %[control:editfield:4e66]{"position":[14,15]}

% Define the lift force:
F = F_bar * u.N;
F = unitConvert(F,SIunits);

  CheckUnitsParameters(g,omega,m,D) %[control:button:3241]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_44FC79A7] ### Determine the number of fundamental dimensions
%[text] 
%[text] Each measurement of the parameters is now associated with their units in the SI unit system. 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:7f65) **Try.** Modify the following edit field to add the units to the other parameters.
% Gather all the units together in a vector:
unitsUsed = findUnits([rho, g, omega, D, m, F])
  %[control:button:3f2e]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%[text] The Ingenuity design comprises six variables; however, these parameters only use three fundamental dimensions, mass $M${"altText":"M"}, length $L${"altText":"L"} and time $T${"altText":"T"}. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_CACD5516] ### Buckingham\*\*-\*\*$\\pi${"altText":"\pi"} **theorem**
%[text] 
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:1285) **Definition.** A physical problem involving $n${"altText":"n"} variables involving $p${"altText":"p"} fundamental dimensions can be expressed as a function of $n-p${"altText":"n-p"} dimensionless quantities.
%[text] In the case of the Ingenuity rover, we have $n=6${"altText":"n=6"} and $p=3${"altText":"p=3"}; therefore, the helicopter can be characterized using $n-p=6-3=3${"altText":"n-p=6-3=3"} dimensionless parameters. The Buckingham-$\\pi${"altText":"\pi"} theorem comes from the Greek letter $\\pi${"altText":"\pi"} because dimensionless numbers are commonly denoted using the letter $\\pi${"altText":"\pi"}.
%[text] The problem:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f(F,\\rho,g,\\omega,m,D) = 0${"altText":"f(F,\rho,g,\omega,m,D) = 0"}
%[text] This can be rewritten as:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\phi(\\pi\_1,\\pi\_2,\\pi\_3) = 0${"altText":"\phi(\pi\_1,\pi\_2,\pi\_3) = 0"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C7981B34] #### Select repeating variables
%[text] 
%[text] We are selecting repeating variables that will appear in $(\\pi\_1,\\pi\_2,\\pi\_3)${"altText":"(\pi\_1,\pi\_2,\pi\_3)"}. These variables must have units, the units must be different, and their units must cover every dimension:
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:468b) **Exercise.** Select the repeating variable.
CheckRepeatingVariable(...
    sym("None"),... %[control:dropdown:50e2]{"position":[5,16]}
    sym("None"),... %[control:dropdown:8db5]{"position":[5,16]}
    sym("None")); %[control:dropdown:307e]{"position":[5,16]}

  %[control:button:3984]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_176B6C08] #### Define dimension scale
%[text] 
%[text] Many combinations of repeating variables are possible and would lead to the same results. In the rest of the script, we will consider a single set of repeating variables: $\[D,\\omega,\\rho\]${"altText":"\[D,\omega,\rho\]"}. Now that we have defined the repeating variables, we can express a characteristic scale for each fundamental dimension involved in the problem.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:16ab) **Try.** Click the following button to define the characteristic length scale.
  %[control:button:7e66]{"position":[1,2]}
L_scale = D
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:0284) **Exercise.** Similarly, define the mass and time scales only using the repeating variables.
% Your code goes here"
M_scale = rho*D^3;
T_scale = 1/omega;

% Check answer:
 CheckDimensionScale(M_scale,T_scale) %[control:button:0ad6]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_58DDB949] #### Scale the problem variables
%[text] 
%[text] We can now define the dimensionless group as a function of the non-repeating variables $\[F,g,m\]${"altText":"\[F,g,m\]"} and the characteristic scale of the fundamental dimensions specified in the previous section.
%[text] 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:8bca) **Try.** Run the following section to define the dimensional scale for length.
 pi_1 = F / (1) %[control:editfield:8519]{"position":[14,15]}
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_AB65D5EF] ![decorative](text:image:7aef) **Reflect.** Notice that we can verify that it is a dimensionless number because no units are showing in the results.
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:1f0d) **Exercise.** Reduce the other problem variables to their dimensionless form.
% Your code goes in the following edit fields:
pi_2 = g / (1) %[control:editfield:635e]{"position":[13,14]}
pi_3 = m / (1) %[control:editfield:65bc]{"position":[13,14]}

% Check Answer:
  CheckDiemensionalGroup(pi_2,pi_3) %[control:button:92c4]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A7403976] ### Experimental study
%[text] 
%[text] A prototype of the Ingenuity copter was tested on Earth. The helicopter mass was fixed to 1.6 $\\text{kg}${"altText":"\text{kg}"}, and five rotors were tested with radius $R = \[1, 3, 10, 30, 100\]${"altText":"R = \[1, 3, 10, 30, 100\]"} $\\text{cm}${"altText":"\text{cm}"}. Five different rotor rotation speeds were considered $\\omega = \[1,3,10,30,100\]${"altText":"\omega = \[1,3,10,30,100\]"} $\\frac{\\text{rad}}{\\text{s}}${"altText":"\frac{\text{rad}}{\text{s}}"}. The air density on the day of the experiment was 1.2 $\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{m}^3}${"altText":"\frac{\text{kg}}{\text{m}^3}"} and the local gravitational acceleration is considered equal to 9.81 $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}^2}${"altText":"\frac{\text{m}}{\text{s}^2}"}.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:4b90) **Try.** Define the fixed parameters.
rho = 1.2;
g = 9.81;
m = 1.6;
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:1c1f) **Try.** Load the experiment results.
  %[control:button:0502]{"position":[1,2]}
load Ingenuity.mat
ExperimentResult
%[text] 
%%
%[text] We can now compute the value of the dimensionless group for each experimental result.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:8f16) **Try.** Compute $\\pi\_1${"altText":"\pi\_1"} 
  %[control:button:86b1]{"position":[1,2]}

ExperimentResult.pi_1 = ExperimentResult.F./...
    ((2*ExperimentResult.R).^4.*ExperimentResult.Omega.^2.*ExperimentResult.Rho)
%[text] Recall that the variables you defined were,
displayFormula(["'pi_1 = '" , string(pi_1), "'   and   '","'pi_2 = '", string(pi_2),"'   and   '", "'pi_3 = '", string(pi_3)])
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:36f3) **Exercise.** Similarly\*\*,\*\* Compute the ratio $\\pi\_2${"altText":"\pi\_2"} and $\\pi\_3${"altText":"\pi\_3"} as defined in the [previous section.](internal:M_AB65D5EF)
% Your code goes here:
ExperimentResult.pi_2(:) = 0;
ExperimentResult.pi_3(:) = 0;

% Check answer:
  CheckPiExperiment(ExperimentResult) %[control:button:1fd2]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_03FA4707] ### Design conclusion
%[text] 
%[text] The experimental results demonstrate that the first dimensionless ratio is constant:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\pi\_1 = \\frac{F}{D^4 \\omega^2 \\rho} = 0.0034${"altText":"\pi\_1 = \frac{F}{D^4 \omega^2 \rho} = 0.0034"}
%[text] The engineering group settles on a rotor diameter of 1.2 $\\text{m}${"altText":"\text{m}"}, the atmospheric density on Mars is 0.02 $\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{m}^3}${"altText":"\frac{\text{kg}}{\text{m}^3}"}, the force required for take-off should be 10% greater than the gravitational force felt by the helicopter, the mass of Ingenuity is 1.8 $\\text{kg}${"altText":"\text{kg}"} and the local acceleration due to gravity is 3.712 $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}^2}${"altText":"\frac{\text{m}}{\text{s}^2}"}.
%[text] 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:67ec) **Try.** Define the minimal rotor rotation speed to operate on Mars.
  %[control:button:96c9]{"position":[1,2]}

% Define the problem parameters:
syms F D omega rho
pi_1 = F/(D^4*omega^2*rho) == 0.0034
pi_1 = subs(pi_1, [F D rho], [1.1*3.712*1.8 1.2 0.02])
omega = solve(pi_1,omega,"PrincipalValue",true)*u.rad/u.s
omega = rewrite(omega,u.rpm)
omega = vpa(omega,5)
%[text] 
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:02ba) **Reflect.** Compare the result from our analysis with the [Ingenuity specification](https://mars.nasa.gov/files/mars2020/MarsHelicopterIngenuity_FactSheet.pdf).
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0536FE82] ## Reynolds number
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Illustration pipe flow](text:image:9988)
%[text] The head loss in the horizontal pipe flow can be expressed as a problem with six variables:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f(\\Delta P, D, L, \\rho,\\mu,v) = 0${"altText":"f(\Delta P, D, L, \rho,\mu,v) = 0"}
%[text] Where $\\Delta P${"altText":"\Delta P"} is the pressure drop, $D${"altText":"D"} is the pipe diameter, $L${"altText":"L"} is the pipe length, $\\rho${"altText":"\rho"} is the fluid density, $\\mu${"altText":"\mu"} is the fluid dynamic viscosity, and $v${"altText":"v"} is the velocity. 
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:42ca) **Try.** Define the parameter of the problem.
syms D_bar v_bar rho_bar Delta_P_bar mu_bar L_bar
u = symunit;
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:8822) **Try.** Define the units of these parameters and check the dimensions used.
D = D_bar * u.m;
v = v_bar * u.m/u.s;
rho = rho_bar * u.kg/u.m^3;
Delta_P = Delta_P_bar * u.Pa;
Delta_P = unitConvert(Delta_P,SIunits);
mu = mu_bar * u.kg/u.m/u.s;
L = L_bar * u.m;
unitsUsed = findUnits([D,v,rho,Delta_P,mu,L])
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:1949) **Exercise.** Using the Buckingham-Pi theorem, how many dimensionless ratios are needed to characterize the problem?
CheckNumDimRatio(0) %[control:spinner:1b73]{"position":[18,19]}
  %[control:button:72f9]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] The number of variables is $n=6${"altText":"n=6"}, and the number of dimensions is $p=3${"altText":"p=3"}. Therefore, we can define $n-p=3${"altText":"n-p=3"} dimensionless ratio to characterize the problem. 
%%
%[text] We select $D\n${"altText":"D "}, $\\rho${"altText":"\rho"}, and $v${"altText":"v"} as repeating variables. The first dimensionless ratio is expressed in terms of $L${"altText":"L"}:
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:2e65) **Exercise.** Define the first dimensionless ratio based on $L${"altText":"L"}.
  %[control:button:3e75]{"position":[1,2]}
% Your code goes here:
pi_1 = L / D

% Check answer:
CheckPi1(pi_1)
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:6763) **Exercise.** Define the first dimensionless ratio based on $\\Delta P${"altText":"\Delta P"}.
  %[control:button:302f]{"position":[1,2]}

% Your code goes here:
pi_2 = Delta_P / (rho*v^2)

% Check answer:
CheckPi2(pi_2)
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:027b) **Exercise.** Define the third dimensionless ratio based on $\\mu${"altText":"\mu"}.
  %[control:button:4828]{"position":[1,2]}

% Your code goes here:
pi_3 = mu / (rho*D*v)

% Check answer:
CheckPi3(pi_3)
%%
%[text] We can rewrite the problem as:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\pi\_3 = \\phi(\\pi\_1,\\pi\_2)${"altText":"\pi\_3 = \phi(\pi\_1,\pi\_2)"}
%[text] The Reynolds number is the most common dimensionless number encountered in fluid mechanics. It is used to predict the fluid flow behavior. It describes if the flow is laminar (low-Reynolds number) or turbulent (high-Reynolds number). It is calculated using the velocity, a length scale, density, and viscosity. You can find more about the Reynolds number in the case of pipe flow in [InternalFlow.m](file:.\\InternalFlow.m).
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_EBA1407D] ## Other common dimensionless groups
%[text] 
%[text] Many dimensionless groupings are commonly used depending on the nature of the problem. While the Reynolds number is the most common one that you might encounter, you could find reference to the following dimensionless groupings:
%[text] - [Darcy friction factor:](file:.\\InternalFlow.m) $\\text{C}\_\\text{f}${"altText":"\text{C}\_\text{f}"}
%[text] - Drag coefficient: $c\_d = \\frac{2F\_d}{\\rho v^2 A}${"altText":"c\_d = \frac{2F\_d}{\rho v^2 A}"}
%[text] - Euler number: $\\text{Eu} = \\frac{\\Delta p}{\\rho V^2}${"altText":"\text{Eu} = \frac{\Delta p}{\rho V^2}"}
%[text] - Froude number: $\\text{Fr} = \\frac{g L^3}{\\nu^2}${"altText":"\text{Fr} = \frac{g L^3}{\nu^2}"}
%[text] - Mach number: $\\text{Ma} = \\frac{v}{c}${"altText":"\text{Ma} = \frac{v}{c}"}
%[text] - Peclet number: $\\text{Pe} = \\frac{Lu}{D}${"altText":"\text{Pe} = \frac{Lu}{D}"}
%[text] - Pressure coefficient: $C\_p = \\frac{p-p\_\\infty}{\\frac{1}{2}\\rho\_\\infty V\_\\infty^2}${"altText":"C\_p = \frac{p-p\_\infty}{\frac{1}{2}\rho\_\infty V\_\infty^2}"} \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B1A242BC] ## Further exploration
%[text] 
%[text] The experimental data presented in this script were generated using the new [rotor blockset](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2023a/aeroblks/rotor.html) in Simulink. The block itself uses dimensionless number to simulate the lift generated by a rotor: the lift $(C\_L)${"altText":"(C\_L)"} and torque coefficient $(C\_Q)${"altText":"(C\_Q)"}. 
